From: Artem Butusov <art.sormy@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] regulator: pwm: an init voltage wins over the boot-on duty

Rockchip's regulator-init-microvolt sets a rail's voltage at enable,
but only while its PWM duty is still 0. pwm_regulator_init_boot_on
runs first and, for a boot-on rail on an inverse-polarity PWM, sets
the duty to the whole period: the init voltage is skipped and the rail
comes up at its maximum, 1006 mV on the HK1's vdd_logic and 1148 mV on
the M2 Plus's vdd_gpu, until a consumer asks for less. Leave the duty
alone where an init voltage is given.

Signed-off-by: Artem Butusov <art.sormy@gmail.com>
---
diff --git a/drivers/regulator/pwm-regulator.c b/drivers/regulator/pwm-regulator.c
--- a/drivers/regulator/pwm-regulator.c
+++ b/drivers/regulator/pwm-regulator.c
@@ -338,7 +338,8 @@
 {
 	struct pwm_state pstate;
 
-	if (!init_data->constraints.boot_on || drvdata->enb_gpio)
+	/* an init voltage is set at enable, but only while the duty is still 0 */
+	if (!init_data->constraints.boot_on || drvdata->enb_gpio || drvdata->init_uv)
 		return 0;
 
 	pwm_get_state(drvdata->pwm, &pstate);
